A: What you probably observe is a bug in either Pex or in the MS Test utility. Running your tests in Release mode could indeed cause your tests to fail. Pex requires the PexSettings attribute and the [PexMethod], [PexClass] attributes on the test method for it to be able to generate test cases in Debug mode but the same cannot be guaranteed in Release mode. In some cases, Pex might not be able to generate comprehensive test cases in Release mode, therefore failing your tests.
Therefore, I would recommend running your tests in Debug mode even in Release configuration and see if that resolves the issue.
Here is a link which covers the issue
https://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cogsvc/professionalprogram/2011/fall/pldi11-preregistration.pdf
One more point to note:
The ArrayList class is not supported by Pex as well, as it is generic and lacks the necessary contracts for Pex. The best practice to use is to replace the ArrayList with List<T> where T is a class / structure / primitive as it has better support from Pex.
So, you should use the List<int> along with int instead of ArrayList and int.
Note: The assumption about the MaxValue could be incorrect depending on the type. Check if PexTestCase.cs in your solution has line "#define TESTCASE_MAXSIZE" if it's not there then add it. If it is there, then you need to modify PEXSettings according to your requirement.
Hope this helps you. If you need more insights, please feel free to ask.

A: I faced similar issue when I tried to run test cases in Release mode, it may be a bug. Try running the tests in Debug mode instead.
The test cases are generated at runtime based on the PEXSettings in the [PexMethod] attribute but may not work correctly when the testing environment is in Release mode. It depends on the PexSettings attributes set and configurations of your system. 
If Pex cannot generate test cases, you can use [PexMethodUnderTest] with your test methods, to generate test cases for an individual method. Or [PexClassUnderTest] to target your entire class and generate test cases for all public and protected methods in the class.
If you try running the tests in Debug mode and get failures, try to reproduce the test cases, run the test cases manually and see if you get the same error results. You may not be able to get the identical results, but at least something might be executing.
Unfortunately, running automated testing in Release mode doesn't work properly with Parameterized Unit Testing and Pex in most cases. If you need to do that, try to run tests in Debug mode.

