I have a large size of test class and cannot paste all methods here.
How I can modify my test-cases to add precondition for [PexMethod, PexAllowedCalls]? I've tried to add preconditions before the [PexMethod, PexAllowedCalls] method but without success.

A: I am not sure that the ArrayList has all its properties ready for the Pex to observe. 
You should add your assumptions in the Arrange phase. 
For example:
public void PexYourTest(
    [PexAssumeNotNull] ArrayList list,
    [PexAssumeValid] int value)
{
    Assume.True(list.Count < 10 /* any condition for pre-condition */);
    //Arrange
    var originalCount = list.Count;

    //Act
    int result = list.Add(value);

    // Assert  
    Assert.True(list.Count == originalCount + 1);
}

This code will check that the count of ArrayList does not exceed 10.
If it's still not working, you might need to manually mock or inject dependencies for observing the behavior of ArrayList correctly in your test.

Regarding your question, you can set precondition before the test methods as you did in your ArrayListContractTest. But if you are unable to reach this point, you can use [PexMethod] annotation to create a test for a single method and check pre-conditions before the method execution.

If you still face challenges, consider asking another question with more context and specific details about your problem. This should assist you better in understanding and resolving the issues.

A: You can apply preconditions by using Assumptions in your testcases. Assumption are used to set up preconditions which are typically boolean conditions.
E.g. You can try something as follows:
public void YourTestCase([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList list, [PexAssumeValid] int value)
{
    Assume.True(list.Count <= 10); // Setting up preconditions
    // Rest of your test case...
}

In this case, the test case will only be run if list.Count is less or equal to 10. 
Remember to set the appropriate assumptions for your data in your test.

A: Precondition is not a concept available in Pex, it is a way for the user to manually control the state of the testing environment. 

A: I might be misunderstanding your question but how about using [PexExpectedException] attribute to define which exception is expected during testing? This attribute is used on methods in the .NET Framework code that are part of your specification.
The Pex engine will analyze the exception being thrown and will verify if it is as expected or not.

A: Just for reference, I think the way to add preconditions for PexMethods is through the use of the [PexAssumeValid] attribute before any parameter that should respect some assumptions.
For instance, if we have an ArrayList object and add a number which is bigger than the range we know can accept, this would raise a precondition error and the test wouldn't be run:

```csharp
[PexMethod]
public void AddExample([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList list, [PexAssumeValid]int value)
{
    Assume.True(value <= 100);
    list.Add(value);
}
```

If value is bigger than 100, the test wouldn't be run.

You could also restrict the possible values that could get passed into a method with [PexInlineData] or [PexAllowedData]

```csharp
